        <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold leading-tight text-gray-900 md:text-5xl">
            Odoo Invoicing: Automate Your Accounting from Sales to Collections
        </h1>

        <p class="text-lg leading-8 text-gray-500">
            Discover how Odoo Invoicing helps businesses streamline billing,
            automate accounting workflows, manage payments, and improve cash
            flow visibility by connecting invoices with the entire sales
            process.
        </p>
    </header>

    <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900">
            Why Invoice Automation Matters for Growing Businesses
        </h2>

        <p>
            Invoicing is a critical part of every business operation. However,
            creating invoices manually, tracking payment dates, reconciling
            transactions, and following up on overdue balances can consume
            valuable time. When these activities depend on spreadsheets and
            disconnected applications, errors become more likely and financial
            visibility can suffer.
        </p>

        <p>
            Efficient invoicing goes beyond sending bills to customers.
            Businesses need a reliable process that connects sales orders,
            accounting records, payment tracking, and collection activities. A
            structured workflow helps finance teams reduce administrative work
            while maintaining accurate and accessible financial information.
        </p>

        <p>
            Odoo Invoicing brings these activities into an integrated business
            platform, helping organizations manage customer invoices and connect
            billing operations with sales and accounting processes.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900">
            What Makes Odoo Invoicing Different?
        </h2>

        <p>
            Odoo connects invoicing with applications such as Sales, Accounting,
            Inventory, and other business tools. Instead of maintaining separate
            records for quotations, orders, invoices, and payments, businesses
            can create connected workflows that reduce duplicate data entry and
            improve coordination between departments.
        </p>

        <p>
            For example, a sales team can prepare a quotation, confirm a
            customer order, and generate an invoice through the configured sales
            workflow. Finance teams can then manage payment status and
            accounting entries using the relevant Odoo applications and
            settings.
        </p>

        <p>
            The available functionality depends on the Odoo version, installed
            applications, localization, and configuration. Understanding these
            requirements before implementation helps ensure the invoicing
            process supports the company's financial and operational needs.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="space-y-4">
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900">
            Automate Invoices from Sales Orders
        </h2>

        <p>
            Connecting sales and invoicing helps businesses reduce the gap
            between closing a deal and requesting payment. With Odoo, companies
            can configure invoicing policies based on their sales processes,
            including invoicing ordered quantities or delivered quantities where
            applicable.
        </p>

        <p>
            Once an order meets the relevant invoicing conditions, users can
            generate customer invoices without recreating the same information
            manually. Customer details, products, quantities, prices, and
            payment terms can flow through the connected process, depending on
            the configuration.
        </p>

        <p>
            This approach helps reduce repetitive administrative tasks and
            inconsistencies between sales documents and invoices. It also gives
            sales and finance teams a clearer view of which transactions require
            billing and which have already progressed to payment.
        </p>
    </section>

